<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A stream arrives in chunks whose boundaries are chosen by the sender, the transport or the
tokenizer, not by the receiving code. When a filter applies a regular expression to each chunk
on its own, a value that starts in one chunk and ends in the next is never seen as a whole:
neither piece matches, so a redaction filter forwards the value unchanged and a blocking filter
lets it through. The failure only shows up when a boundary happens to fall inside a match, so
tests that feed the filter whole strings pass.
</p>
<p>
This query flags a regular expression that can match two or more characters when it is applied
to a value derived from the current chunk alone inside a per-chunk callback: a Node.js
<code>Transform</code> or <code>Writable</code> implementation, a web <code>TransformStream</code>
transformer, a <code>'data'</code> event listener, or the body of a <code>for await</code> loop.
Applying the pattern to the chunk joined with carried text (for example a held-back tail or an
accumulated buffer) is not flagged.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Carry state between chunks. Hold back a tail at least as long as the longest possible match,
prepend it to the next chunk before matching, emit only the part that can no longer be the start
of a match, and flush the tail when the stream ends. Alternatively buffer to a natural boundary
(a line, a message) and apply the pattern once per boundary. For patterns with unbounded
matches, set an explicit cap and treat reaching it as a reportable condition rather than
silently cutting the match.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example the pattern is applied to each chunk separately. An email address
that is split across two chunks, such as <code>jane.do</code> and <code>e@example.com</code>, is
forwarded without redaction.
</p>
<sample src="examples/SplitBoundaryChunkFilterBad.js" />
<p>
The corrected version keeps the last 64 characters of the previous chunk, matches against the
join, and releases the held tail at the end of the stream.
</p>
<sample src="examples/SplitBoundaryChunkFilterGood.js" />
</example>

<references>
<li>CWE-693: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/693.html">Protection Mechanism Failure</a>.</li>
<li>T. Ptacek and T. Newsham, <a href="https://insecure.org/stf/secnet_ids/secnet_ids.html">Insertion, Evasion, and Denial of Service: Eluding Network Intrusion Detection</a>, 1998: the same mechanism at the network layer.</li>
<li>N. Phalak, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22909585">Split-Boundary Leaks in Streaming Guardrails</a>: measurements across streaming redaction filters.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
